      "question": "The last maintenance job on Transformer 1 at site MAIN was supposed to deal with the gassing, and I want to know whether it actually worked.\n\nFind the most recently closed work order on Transformer 1. Give me its number, description, completion date, resolution notes and failure code, and tell me which fault it was meant to fix.\n\nThen look at the oil samples taken after that date, within 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 to 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000. Use hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene and co. Screen hydrogen, acetylene and co for anomalous samples with a proper anomaly detection model rather than a fixed threshold. Separately, run a dissolved gas fault classification on the latest complete sample and compare its fault class with the fault the job was meant to fix.\n\nDecide as follows:\n- If both checks point to trouble, meaning at least one anomaly is flagged and the classification still shows the same fault or a higher-energy one, call the fix ineffective. Create a priority 2 corrective follow-up work order that references the original work order number, and code it with the closest failure code from our catalog.\n- If only one of the two checks points to trouble, don't create a corrective job. Recommend a confirmatory oil resample within 7 days and say which check disagreed.\n- If neither points to trouble, say the fix appears to have held.\n\nIf the closed job was oil filtration, degassing or regeneration, the gas levels will have been reset. In that case, judge the result on the rate of change after the job, not on absolute levels, and say you did so.\n\nIf no work order on Transformer 1 was closed before or during the window, say so, use all samples in the window, and give only the classification and anomaly result without a verdict on any fix. If fewer than 4 samples fall after the completion date, skip the anomaly model and compare the first and last post-job values instead.",
      "answer": "I couldn't give you a verdict on whether the fix worked, because no work order on Transformer 1 has been closed. The classification ran with ratios I worked out by hand. The anomaly screen did not run.\n\n**Most recently closed work order:** none. Transformer 1 at MAIN has two work orders, and neither is closed:\n- **1000052**, \"Oil sample retest (DGA) and insulation check for T2 thermal fault\". Status APPR (approved), failure code FC006 (overheating), no completion date.\n- **1000051**, \"OLTC inspection (suspected contact wear/coking)\". Status WAPPR (waiting for approval), no failure code, no completion date.\n\nThat means there's no completion date, resolution notes or failure code to report, and no fault that a finished job was meant to fix. As you asked, I used all 4 samples in the window (2024-01-15 to 2024-01-18) and am giving only the classification and anomaly results.\n\n**Fault classification (latest complete sample, 2024-01-18):** the DGA interpretation tool failed twice (\"LLM unavailable\"), so I calculated the IEC 60599 ratios myself.\n- Gas levels: H\u2082 2900, CH\u2084 7350, C\u2082H\u2086 5450, C\u2082H\u2084 16600, C\u2082H\u2082 7, CO 33 ppm.\n- C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 = 0.0004, which is below 0.1.\n- CH\u2084/H\u2082 = 2.53, which is above 1.\n- C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 = 3.05, which is between 1 and 4.\n- **Result: T2 thermal fault (300\u2013700 \u00b0C).** The last ratio is near the upper limit of 4, where it would become T3 (above 700 \u00b0C). CO is low, so paper insulation doesn't look involved.\n\n**Anomaly screen (hydrogen, acetylene, CO): not run.** The model catalogue has no anomaly detection models, and the tool only works from a saved data file. I found no such file for these samples and have no tool here to create one. I didn't fall back to a fixed threshold, as you asked. Just for reference, the levels are flat across the four days:\n- H\u2082: 2845 \u2192 2950 \u2192 2800 \u2192 2900\n- C\u2082H\u2082: 7 \u2192 6 \u2192 8 \u2192 7\n- CO: 32 \u2192 30 \u2192 35 \u2192 33\n\n**What this means:** the oil still shows an active T2 overheating fault at very high gas levels. I didn't create any new work order. Approved order 1000052 (DGA retest and insulation check, FC006) already covers this fault.\n\nTo get a real verdict, first close the job that actually fixes the fault, with its resolution notes and failure code. Then either save the post-job samples as a CSV file for the anomaly model, or rerun once the DGA tool is working again.",
      "score": {
